app/ar8030: drop ioctl replies no call is waiting for

io_rpc_cb() took every packet on the ioctl connection as the reply to
whatever call ran last. iose->pbuf keeps pointing at that caller's
output buffer -- usually a stack local -- after the call returned, so a
reply that arrived with no call waiting (a duplicate, or a late one
after a timed-out bb_ioctl_ex()) was memcpy'd by the reader thread into
a dead stack frame of whichever thread made the last call.

Confirmed on the BetaFPV P1 air (core dump, 2026-11-12):
ar8030-transport-tx's 3 s liveness check (BB_GET_STATUS into a 300-byte
stack local) had returned; a second BB_GET_STATUS reply then landed on
the same addresses, which now held the saved frame record of the futex
wait the main thread sat in, and tx returned to "address" 5805000 (the
channel's freq_khz) -- SIGSEGV with no log line, about once an hour,
each time right after the chip logged "SDIO read over but no cb". Every
client of the library (tx, lifecycled, linkctl, rx) shares this path.

Copy and wake only while bs_send_usbpack_and_wait() still waits for
exactly that reqid (it holds psess->mtx everywhere but in its cond
wait), do both under psess->mtx, clear pbuf once the call returns, and
log anything dropped to stderr (stdout is fully buffered in daemons
whose stdout is a file, so a printf would only show up much later).

Verified on the same P1: 14 min after a bind, tx logged "io: dropped
reply req 0x1000000 len 300 sta 0 (no call waiting, last 0x1000000)" --
the reply that used to kill it -- right after the chip's SDIO glitch,
and kept running.

Mirrors builder's own identical patch 0038 (same vendor source).

diff --git a/app/ar8030/session_ioctl.c b/app/ar8030/session_ioctl.c
--- a/app/ar8030/session_ioctl.c
+++ b/app/ar8030/session_ioctl.c
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
 #include "ioctl_tab.h"
 #include "session.h"
 #include "usbpack.h"
+#include <pthread.h>
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <string.h>
@@ -19,7 +20,30 @@
 static int io_rpc_cb(struct BB_HANDLE* phd, struct usbpack* pack, struct BASE_SESSION* psess)
 {
     IOCTL_SESSION* iose = container_of(psess, IOCTL_SESSION, base);
-    iose->sta           = pack->sta;
+
+    /* A reply is only ever copied into the caller's output buffer while
+     * that caller still waits for it in bs_send_usbpack_and_wait(), which
+     * holds psess->mtx except inside its cond wait. Before this, every
+     * packet on the connection was taken as the reply to whatever ran
+     * last: iose->pbuf still pointed at the previous caller's stack
+     * buffer after it returned, so a reply arriving with no call waiting
+     * (a duplicate, or a late one after a timed-out call) was memcpy'd
+     * into a dead stack frame by this reader thread. Confirmed on the P1
+     * air (core dump): a BB_GET_STATUS reply landed in the frame
+     * ar8030-transport-tx's 3 s liveness check had left, on top of the
+     * saved frame record of the futex wait the main thread sat in, and tx
+     * jumped to "address" 5805000 (the channel's freq_khz) -- SIGSEGV
+     * about once an hour, each time right after the chip logged "SDIO
+     * read over but no cb". */
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&psess->mtx);
+    if (!psess->wakeup_need || psess->wakeup_set || pack->reqid != (uint32_t)iose->reqid) {
+        int waiting = psess->wakeup_need && !psess->wakeup_set;
+        pthread_mutex_unlock(&psess->mtx);
+        fprintf(stderr, "io: dropped reply req %#x len %d sta %d (%s %#x)\n", pack->reqid, pack->datalen,
+                pack->sta, waiting ? "waiting for" : "no call waiting, last", iose->reqid);
+        return 0;
+    }
+    iose->sta = pack->sta;
     if (iose->pbuf && pack->datalen) {
         /* pack->datalen is whatever the daemon (and, upstream of it, the
          * chip's own RTOS firmware over whichever physical transport)
@@ -46,7 +70,10 @@
         }
         memcpy(iose->pbuf, pack->datapack, copylen);
     }
-    bs_weakup(psess);
+    /* bs_weakup() without taking psess->mtx a second time. */
+    psess->wakeup_set = 1;
+    pthread_cond_broadcast(&psess->cv);
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&psess->mtx);
     return 0;
 }
 
@@ -127,6 +154,11 @@
 
     int ret = bs_send_usbpack_and_wait(&iose->base, &pack, timeout);
 
+    /* The output buffer is the caller's and goes out of scope now. */
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&iose->base.mtx);
+    iose->pbuf = NULL;
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&iose->base.mtx);
+
     if (ret) {
         printf("ioctl fail , remote exit\n");
         return -1;
